5</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Richardson</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>What Patients Expect From Complementary Therapy: A Qualitative Study</article-title><source>American Journal of Public Health</source><year>2004</year><volume>94</volume><fpage>1049</fpage><lpage>1053</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15249314</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2105/AJPH.94.6.1049</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Murray</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Shepherd</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Alternative or additional medicine? An exploratory study in general practice</article-title><source>Social Science and Medicine</source><year>1993</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>983</fpage><lpage>988</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8235745</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0277-9536(93)90432-4</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Freymann</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Rennie</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Bates</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Nebel</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Heinrich</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Knowledge and use of complementary and alternative medicine among British undergraduate pharmacy students</article-title><source>Pharmacy World and Science</source><year>2006</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>13</fpage><lpage>18</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16691362</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s11096-005-2221-z</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wadlow</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Peringer</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Retrospective survey of patients of practitioners of traditional Chinese acupuncture in the UK</article-title><source>Complementary Therapies in Medicine</source><year>1996</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0965-2299(96)80048-3</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Furnham</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Forey</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The attitudes, behaviors and beliefs of patients of conventional vs. complementary (alternative) medicine</article-title><source>Journal of Clinical Psychology</source><year>1994</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>458</fpage><lpage>469</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8071452</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/1097-4679(199405)50:3&#x0003c;458::AID-JCLP2270500318&#x0003e;3.0.CO;2-V</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Furnham</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Does experience of the 'occult' predict use of complementary medicine? Experience of, and beliefs about, both complementary medicine and ways of telling the future</article-title><source>Complementary Therapies in Medicine</source><year>2000</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>266</fpage><lpage>2</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11098203</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1054/ctim.2000.0352</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Emslie</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Campbell</surname><given-names>MK</given-names></name><name><sur